                        <sourceDesc>
                            <p>The ETCBC4b text is based on the printed edition of the Biblia
                                Hebraica Stuttgartensia, editio quinta emendata (1997). This
                                edition, published by the German Bible Society, represents the text
                                found in the oldest complete manuscript of the Hebrew Bible / Old
                                Testament, the so-called Codex Leningradensis (Codex Firkovich B 19
                                A). Although it cannot be taken for granted that this manuscript,
                                and hence the scholarly edition that represents it, always contains
                                the best (“original”) reading and although we have older manuscripts
                                for various parts of the Bible (Dead Sea Scrolls, some other codices
                                that cover only part of the Hebrew Bible), taking this edition as
                                basis for the electronic text was a natural step because of its
                                central role as *the* scholarly edition of the Old Testament that is
                                almost exclusively used by biblical scholars and that is the basis
                                for almost all Bible translations in modern languages as far as they
                                translate the Hebrew text. The linguistic features of ETCBC4b have
                                been provided by the Eep Talstra Centre for Bible and Computing. At
                                the moment of depositing, this version is still in beta. The data
                                deposited is the newest data that was available at the date of
                                depositing. This LAF resource has been generated from the contents
                                of an Emdros database However, not all data that the ETCBC group is
                                producing, is published in this form. We have made a selection of
                                some extra data, and have added that as an extra LAF annotation
                                file, which can be used in conjunction with the LAF resource
                                originating from the Emdros database. We refer to the ETCBC
                                organization on Github for more examples of work done on with
                                LAF-Fabric operating on ETCBC4.</p>
